 "overview": {
  "summary": "The basic building block of the calibrator: every other assembly makes its ac and dc voltages and currents out of A11's precision dc voltage, and A11's own adc is what characterises the whole instrument during calibration. It is a pulse-width-modulated DAC rather than a resistor-ladder one. Two square waves of fixed amplitude and software-controlled duty cycle -- a coarse channel switched between the 13 V reference and 0 V, and a fine channel switched between about 0.78 mV and 0 V -- are averaged by a five-pole filter on the plug-in A11A1 SIP whose dominant pole sits near 10 Hz, which is 120 dB of rejection at the 190 Hz switching rate. What comes out is a dc voltage of 0 to 22 V of either polarity with 0.309 mV of coarse resolution and 18.5 nV of fine. Accuracy comes from two heated hybrids: HR5, a 13 V reference of two cascaded temperature-compensated ref amps held at 62 °C, and HR6, a precision op amp and its ratio-matched network held at 50 °C. Around them sit the duty-cycle control (82C54 timer U6, optocouplers U12 and U13, flip-flop U14 and a watchdog that pins the filter input at REFCOM if the 8 MHz clock stops), the output stage and its 60 mA current limit, three correction circuits -- series linearity, shunt linearity and negative offset -- the latching output relays, and the calibration hardware: a 22-bit recirculating-remainder adc (U25) with a gain-of-11 null amplifier in front of it. Because that adc reads the diagnostic line for every other analog board, an A11 fault presents as faults everywhere; confirm A11 before chasing anything it measures.",
  "connectors": "P401 and P402, DIN 41612 type C, 64-pin right angle, into J401 and J402 on the Analog Motherboard.",
  "sections": "§2-100 … §2-117",
  "note": "A11 runs on more separate rails than any other analog board -- +30 FR1, FR1 COM, +5 FR1, -18 FR1, +5 FR1R, +30 FR2, FR2 COM, -5 FR2 on P401, and ±17 S, S COM, ±44 S, ±5 LH, LH COM, +5 RLH and RLH COM on P402 -- each entering through a 6-turn choke with 0.22 µF bypasses. A rail fault presents as a DAC fault. It also carries three separate commons on purpose: SCOM (TP1), REFCOM (TP3) and ADCCOM (TP12) are isolated so reference currents do not return through the adc, and substituting one for another gives a plausible but wrong reading."
 },
