 "caveats": [
  {
   "title": "One procedure here, four sections in the manual",
   "text": "Chapter 5 prints A11's troubleshooting as §5-13 (16 steps), §5-14 Duty-cycle Control Circuit (13 steps), §5-15 ADC Circuit (14 steps) and §5-16 Buffered Reference SIP Assembly (2 steps), each numbered from 1. The app shows one procedure of 45 steps, so §5-14's steps are 17-29 here, §5-15's are 30-43 and §5-16's are 44-45; §5-13's are unchanged. Each step still says which section it was printed in. This is not the manual's numbering and it is not claimed to be: unlike A14, where §5-19 step 6 refers forward to 'step 10' and so implies a single sequence, A11's sections hand off to each other by name -- §5-13 steps 3, 4 and 5 all say 'skip to Duty Cycle Control Circuit'. The renumbering exists because the viewer walks one procedure per board. Expectations authored against §5-14, §5-15 and §5-16 cite the combined number and give the printed number in their note, so nothing here can attach an expectation to the wrong step.",
   "refs": []
  },
  {
   "title": "§5-16 troubleshoots a different assembly",
   "text": "§5-16 is printed inside A11's troubleshooting but every measurement and every suspect in it is on the plug-in DAC Buffered Reference SIP (A11A2). Its U1A, U2A, Q3, Q4, U1B, U2B, Q10, Q11, TP1 and TP2 are the SIP's; A11 has parts of all those names and they are different parts. The section is kept because a technician following §5-13 arrives at it, but its designator links are suppressed and the two measurements are carried as probe points that name the SIP. The same applies to §5-13 step 9, which measures TP1 on the DAC Filter SIP (A11A1) while A11's own TP1 is SCOM.",
   "refs": [
    "A1",
    "A2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Table 6-12 omits K6 and K7",
   "text": "The 1996 parts list prints 'K3,K5,K8  RELAY,ARMATURE,2 FORM C,5V,LATCH  769307  3'. The Rev 9 list prints the same part as 'K 3, 5-8' with a quantity of 5, and the drawing, the sheet 1 relay legend ('2FCL BOTTOM VIEW  K3, K5-K8') and the sheet 6 drive circuit all show K6 and K7 as real relays -- §5-15 step 12 tells you to check them by name when TP9 reads wrong. K6 and K7 are carried here from the Rev 9 list and marked as Rev-9-only for that reason. They are not new parts; the 1996 row simply lost two designators and its quantity was not corrected.",
   "refs": [
    "K3",
    "K5",
    "K6",
    "K7",
    "K8"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Fault code 2809 names R70; the divider is R79",
   "text": "Code 2809 says the 1 V and 10 V readings are 'divided by R70 and R84 on the DAC'. §5-15 step 1 and §2-114 both say R79 and R84, and the schematic bears them out: sheet 5 shows DAC HI DIAG entering through R79 (100 kΩ 1 %) with R84 (4.7 kΩ) below it, a ratio of 4.49 % -- which is why the same step expects 0.45 V from a 10 V output and why 2809's own nominal gain is 22.13. R70 is a 20 kΩ 0.1 % resistor in the adc amplifier and has nothing to do with the divider. Read 2809 as R79.",
   "refs": [
    "R79",
    "R84",
    "R70"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The two parts lists disagree about the zeners, and about R23",
   "text": "Eleven zener rows fail to join on stock number between the two revisions, and the pattern is consistent: the 1996 list specifies surface-mount SOT-23 parts and the Rev 9 list the leaded 0.4 W 1N9xx equivalents the earlier boards were built with -- VR4 is 876433 (SOT-23) against 810325 (1N967B), VR7/VR16 831230 against 811927, and so on. Those are recorded as 'as built' and either part works. One row is a real value change rather than a package change: R23 is 6.8 Ω in the 1996 list and 10 Ω in the Rev 9 list. R23 is the current sense in the output buffer's supply lead that sets the 60 mA short-circuit limit, so the two revisions limit at different currents. HR5 also changed stock number, 761338 to 893334.",
   "refs": [
    "R23",
    "VR4",
    "VR7",
    "VR16",
    "HR5"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "TP8's three checks do not agree on the common",
   "text": "§5-13 step 12 measures TP8 '(common to TP12)'; steps 14 and 16 measure the same point '(common to TP3)'. TP12 is ADCCOM and TP3 is REFCOM MECCA POINT, and §2-115 is explicit that the two are deliberately separate so reference currents do not return through the adc. The DAC output is defined against REFCOM, so TP3 is followed here and step 12's TP12 is recorded rather than acted on. The difference is small enough that a ±0.25 V check will not show it -- which is exactly why it is worth knowing before making a fine measurement.",
   "refs": [
    "TP3",
    "TP8",
    "TP12"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Three names for each SIP",
   "text": "§2-100 calls them A11A1 and A11A2. Table 6-12 lists them as A1 and A2, which is how they appear in this dataset because that is the designator on the parts row. Schematic sheet 1 labels the same two boards A93 (drawing 5700A-4043) and A94 (drawing 5700A-4044), and the Manual Status Information table on page 6-4 lists them a fourth way, as A93 and A95. They are all the same two parts, stock 761395 and 764639.",
   "refs": [
    "A1",
    "A2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Q27 and J21 are on the board but in no parts list",
   "text": "The locator drawing prints Q27 between Q25 and C9 with no component outline around it -- an unstuffed position -- and it appears in neither the 1996 nor the Rev 9 list. J21 is the large rectangular outline between C111 and C110 where the DAC Filter SIP plugs in; it too has no parts-list line, the sheets naming the same connections P21/P22 and J2A/J2B instead. Neither is offered as a part here, and a reader who finds those legends on the board and cannot search for them is not missing anything.",
   "refs": [
    "A1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Table 6-12's second 10 kΩ row is printed with a quantity of zero",
   "text": "The 1996 list gives 'R19-21,R29,R31,R41,R48 ... 746610 ... 10' -- seven designators against a quantity of ten -- and then 'R82,R115,R116 ... 746610 ... 0'. The ten covers both rows; the second row's own quantity is left at zero rather than repeated. Nothing is missing, but any count taken from the quantity column of this table will be wrong by three.",
   "refs": [
    "R19",
    "R82",
    "R115",
    "R116"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The revision comes from the photograph, not the drawing",
   "text": "The board is silkscreened 'DAC ASSY 761122 REV' with the letter left blank and stamped per board. The locator drawing carries the same blank field -- it prints 'DAC REV' at the right-hand edge with nothing after it -- so unlike A18 there is no revision on the artwork. The photographed board reads REV P. The Manual Status Information table gives the assembly's revision level as 104, which is a different numbering again.",
   "refs": []
  },
  {
   "title": "The schematic's S and 5 are nearly the same glyph",
   "text": "Figure 7-11's lettering renders a capital S almost identically to a 5, and this board's net names are full of both. '+17S' reads as '+175', 'SCOM' as '5COM', '+5 FR1' as '+S FR1'. The rails are +17 S, -17 S, +44 S, -44 S and their buffered versions +17 SB and -17 SB; the 5 V rails are +5 FR1, +5 FR1R, -5 FR2, ±5 LH and +5 RLH. Worth knowing before reading a net name off a sheet, and worth remembering when reviewing anything a machine read off one.",
   "refs": []
  }
 ],
